TEXT 2928 4232 Left 2 !.inc CD4000_v.lib
TEXT 3240 4296 Left 2 !.param VDD1=3V
TEXT 2928 4256 Left 2 !.inc CD4011B.cir
TEXT 2928 4280 Left 2 !.inc CD4071B.cir
TEXT 2928 4304 Left 2 !.inc CD4081B.cir
TEXT 3240 4272 Left 2 !.tran 0 18000s 0 2ms startup
TEXT 816 -440 Left 2 !.model MYD2 D(Bv=3.3 type=zener)
TEXT 2936 4136 Left 2 ;ATT!!\nComment out CD components that I use that are not CD4013B from cd4000_v lib \nthis avoids problems from overlapping definitions
TEXT -928 1448 Left 2 ;Flip flops' supply voltage (their models \nrequire a source called VDD\nThey will be supplied by VDDR in real life
TEXT -232 1408 Left 2 ;VDDR actually powers VDD of digital components\nWe don't power them directly from VDDRSIG because\nthe CD4000 power consumption values are off.
TEXT -2048 -352 Left 5 ;Input power path
TEXT 1352 -344 Left 5 ;Capdyn input control
TEXT -1208 -336 Left 5 ;Capdyn: main bank and switches
TEXT -3064 768 Left 5 ;Capdyn: automatic switching mechanism
TEXT 1344 136 Left 5 ;Capdyn output control
TEXT 1528 800 Left 2 ;RC filter slows down Q1 signal with respect\n to UVB to trigger CLR upon desired action.
TEXT 3064 1448 Left 2 ;Next task config memorization is implemented in capdyn as a shift register, piloted directly by MCU.\nNot in use for this simulation, but implemented in capdynv2 PCB.\nSee full_v2 to see this part working as it does in the v2 PCB.\nIn v2 PCB, configuration is passed to capdyn's "next task" shift register through these signals:\nD_IN: data line with desired next stage information\nCLK_CNF: clock line for sampling data line, produced by MCU
TEXT -2040 304 Left 5 ;Output power path
TEXT 2920 4072 Left 5 ;Simulation parameters and libraries
TEXT 3440 376 Left 2 ;Instead of OVB_bck, which triggers task\njust when backup reaches 5.5 V \nwe can trigger task whenever\nbackup is in its voltage range by using EN1
TEXT 2096 0 Left 2 ;EN1_CAP triggered high when backup cap first charges up to 5.5V\ntriggered low when backup cap discharges down to 3.85 V (clamping diodes limit)
TEXT 1336 0 Left 2 ;Window type voltage monitor simulator:\nThis lower limit is dictated by clamping diodes at the switches
TEXT 1336 1088 Left 5 ;Capdyn configuration control
TEXT 3064 -56 Left 2 ;INPUT power drive EN1:\nEN1_CAP signals that the backup capacitor is in its range, so capdyn can be safely charged.\nNOTRDY allows power to be redirected to the backup cap when the main bank is charged\nto the desired stage. EN2 allows capdyn to still accumulate energy when load is activated (EN2).
TEXT -2040 1208 Left 2 ;DLR: serial data to be shifted right\nIn our case, always VCC
TEXT -3032 1208 Left 2 ;Window type voltage monitor simulator\nupper switching threshold for capdyn: 3.65\nlower switching threshold for capdyn: 1.80
TEXT 3008 840 Left 3 ;3. EN2_CAP adds additional conditions to capdyn's output activation.\nIt is set when both the main bank is ready (RDY) and the backup \ncapacitor has just reached 5.5 V (OVB_bck).\nIt is reset when either the MCU has finished its operations\nor if main bank becomes discharged (NOTRDY)
TEXT 3456 112 Left 2 ;* In previous versions (v3.1 downwards), instead of \ndetaching the load at UVB_BCK signal, we used a\n comparator to provide a PGOOD signal. \nThis allowed us to extend execution down to 3.1 V_BCK.
TEXT 2928 4328 Left 2 !.inc tlv3691.txt
TEXT 2160 112 Left 2 ;This part of the circuit controls output power flow from the main bank.\nOutput is activated when desired stage is fully charged and backup capacitor is topped up.\nOutput is deactivated if either we overdischarge bank or backup, or if we end operation on MCU side.\nA successfull task should only end due to this latter reason.
TEXT 2928 4400 Left 2 !.param cap=0.0225 decoup=5n pow_inp=10e-4
TEXT -512 1824 Left 5 ;This section is responsible for attaching to CapDYN a load consuming a certain amount of energy.\nTasks are configured as a constant power load for a certain amount of time, yielding the measured \ntask energy consumption in worst case scenario (max supply voltage)
TEXT 3240 4328 Left 2 !.ic V(V_cpdn)=0 V(pow_in)=0 V(VDDRSIG)=2.5 V(V_BCK)=0
TEXT 224 2896 Left 3 ;EOF simulates task duration by delaying POS_EDGE (EN_2 rising edge pulse) of the active task's duration. \nEOF must only be a short peak: if it stays high, we cannot set EN2 at capdyn load control side, as EOF keeps RDY in reset!
TEXT -1688 2728 Left 3 ;EOFR clocks the next task shift register immediately after an EOF is issued (EN2 rising edge, delayed by task time)\nNext task register must be clocked once EOF has had its effect on capdyn output control (EN2).\nFor this purpose, we delay the EOF signal by 50 ms and pilot the task register with this delayed signal, EOFR.\n \nIf we were to pilot task register directly from EOF:\nEOF would not have time to deactivate EN2, causing problems.
TEXT 3176 1376 Left 5 ;Capdyn "next task" shift register
TEXT 1336 1528 Left 2 ;Ti signals tell capdyn at which stage completion (Qi) we want to turn on load output.\nIn PCB: Ti signal is output of next task shift register, which is piloted by D_IN and CLK_CNF of MCU.\nIn this simulation, Ti are produced through a simple task scheduler circuit acting as the MCU + some other logic acting as the next task register
TEXT -3048 1872 Left 3 ;This acts as the MCU's task scheduling algorithm, which sets up a circular sequence of four tasks.\nOnce EN2 is enabled, the current task executed and issues an EOF at the end of the task. Once EOF is issued, we set up the next task.\nIn reality, the MCU will control the task register in capdyn, setting up the next task in the "next task" register.\nFor the sake of the LTspice simulation, we needed the MCu's signals telling us which is the next task - this is what we have done here,\nTo see how the task register is actually implemented, see full_v2
